== The Ferry Steps ==
The ferry backs water off the pilings and leaves you standing on wet boards with a borrowed coat, a solicitor's letter going soft in your pocket, and the whole of the fog coming in behind it like a debt collector. Below your boots the harbour mutters. Above it, somewhere past the church roof, the Marn Point light stands dark for the first night in fifty-one years.

You are the late Mrs Voss's grand-nephew by marriage twice removed, which is how the law puts it. What the letter says is simpler: keep the light burning until the relief boat comes Monday. The Meridian is expected on the young flood tonight, twelve hands and a deck cargo of pit props, and the reef they call the Teeth lies directly under her course.

~ take "storm lantern"
~ take "tin of lamp oil", 2

In your fist: a storm lantern, cold and unlit. Under your arm: two tins of lamp oil, heavy as sleeping cats. It is the last hour of daylight, and the fog has already eaten the far mole.

[[Follow the lane up from the harbour -> The Cliff Foot]]

== The Cliff Foot ==
The lane climbs between net-lofts whose windows glow the colour of weak tea. Rope hangs everywhere, drying in coils that brush your shoulder like something alive. Salt sits on your lips; under it, tar, and under that, fish.

{{if seen("The Salt and Anchor")}}
Light spills again from the Salt and Anchor's windows, and Ivy's laugh comes out flat against the fog.
{{end}}
{{if seen("Harbour Office")}}
The harbour office lamp still burns behind its small panes. Dune keeps late hours.
{{end}}

From up the headland comes a sound you will hear all night and never quite get used to: the sea working the base of the cliffs, patient, enormous, unhurried.

* Knock at the harbour office -> Harbour Office
* Push open the door of the Salt and Anchor -> The Salt and Anchor
* Start up the headland path -> The Headland Path
* (if turns() >= 60) The letter in your pocket feels suddenly heavy. Up the path -> The Headland Path

== Harbour Office ==
Elias Dune receives you with a lamp turned low and a chest he can't stop coughing round. Charts cover his desk like dealt cards, and one chart in particular is weighted at the corner with a brass ashtray: the approaches to Marn Point, the Teeth marked in red ink gone brown with age.

"The Meridian," he says, as if you'd asked. "Captain Enys. Due on the young flood, which puts her abeam of you somewhere about three in the morning, if the fog lets her see her own nose. Twelve hands." He looks at you properly then, the way men look at weather. "Mrs Voss kept that light through worse than this for forty years. You'll do it tonight because it must be done."

He shoves a ledger across for your name. Beside the ledger, on a nail, hangs a key on a wooden tag: LAMP STORE.

* (once) Sign the ledger and take the key from its nail -> The Brass Key
* (once) Ask what's in the steel cabinet behind him -> The Flare Case
[[Take the cliff path north -> The Cliff Foot]]

== The Brass Key ==
Your signature goes into the ledger under forty years of Vosses, and the brass key comes off its nail cold and heavier than it looks. Dune watches you pocket it without comment.

"Lamp store's the iron door at the seaward wall," he says. "Full rack. Don't waste it — the drum won't be replenished till the relief boat." He turns back to his charts, then adds, not quite to you: "Mind the third step up. It's a liar."

~ take "brass key"

[[Back across the office -> Harbour Office]]

== The Flare Case ==
The cabinet opens on a smell of gun oil and old biscuits. Inside: a canvas case, naval stencil half worn away. Inside that: one signal flare, pistol-shaped, red band painted round the barrel, and a waxed note in the coastguard's blunt hand — ONE STAR. RED. FOR USE WHEN ALL ELSE FAILS.

"That's yours if you want it," Dune says. "Cost the parish nothing. Surplus." He coughs. "Though if you're firing it, the night's already gone wrong."

~ take "signal flare"

[[Take the case and thank him -> Harbour Office]]

== The Salt and Anchor ==
Warmth first, then noise: the Anchor is low-ceilinged, thick with pipe smoke and the particular steam of twenty wet coats drying at once. A fire mutters in a black-leaded stove. Behind the bar, a broad woman with forearms like mooring posts is drawing cider and opinions in equal measure. A chalkboard advertises STEW and, underneath, in smaller chalk, NO CREDIT FOR LIGHTHOUSE ARGUMENTS.

Conversations dip as you come in — the stranger, the coat, the letter everyone's already heard about — then rise again.

* (once) Claim the bench closest to the stove -> Warm at the Stove
* (once) Ask the landlady about the Voss family -> Ivy Tells It
[[Drink up and climb while the light lasts -> The Cliff Foot]]

== Warm at the Stove ==
The bench accepts you. Heat finds the small of your back and works outward, loosening joints you hadn't noticed seizing. Someone passes your knee a mug of something scalding and sweet without being asked, which is the harbour's way of wishing you luck.

For ten minutes you simply sit, watching the fire breathe, listening to the building tick. Whatever the night means to do to you, it will do it warmer-hearted than it would have.

~ set nerve = nerve + 1

[[Reluctantly, back to the door -> The Salt and Anchor]]

== Ivy Tells It ==
Ivy Tregale plants a pint in front of you uninvited and leans on the bar.

"You'll be the one up at Marn tonight." It isn't a question. "Voss women kept that light since before this pub had windows. Old Aldous drowned in the gale and his Maren walked up the zigzag every night after, alone, thirty years, rain or rifles." She polishes a glass that was already clean. "Young Edith was the last. Her granddaughter's been staying with me since the funeral. Good girl. Takes it hard, the tower standing empty."

She leans closer, voice dropping under the stove's mutter. "And you'll hear the Gullet buoy tonight. All the old ones say it rings hardest when the Teeth are hungry. Edith never laughed at that, mind. Edith never laughed at much."

[[Finish the pint -> The Salt and Anchor]]

== The Headland Path ==
The path leaves the lane where the gaslight gives out and becomes a rutted trodden spine through gorse. Fog closes from both sides at once, soft and total, and the world shrinks to eight feet of turf with the sea's breathing somewhere below your left boot.

Twice, shapes loom and resolve: a leaning waymark post; a rusted capstan winch abandoned by some contractor forty years dead. Overhead — nothing. The tower is somewhere above you, but the fog has taken it entirely, and with it any sense of how high you've climbed.

Once, faint through the murk, a bell strikes three times, pauses, strikes three more.

[[Where the ground tilts, take the zigzag stair above the surf -> The Zigzag Stair]]
[[Think better of it and go back down to the town -> The Cliff Foot]]

== The Zigzag Stair ==
The stair is iron, bolted to the cliff in switchbacks, with a chain rail that has absorbed a century of salt and grief. Below you, unseen, the surf files its complaints against the rock. The wind arrives in freight loads, one after another, shoving the fog past you in ribbons.

Halfway up, the gulls prove the fog is not empty: one bursts off the ledge beside your head, screaming, and your heart tries to leave by your throat.

* (time=9, timeout -> The Lost Lantern) Grip the chain with both hands and take the next gust head-on -> Ride It Out
* Drop to a crouch and feel for each step with your boot before trusting it -> Chain Crawl

== Ride It Out ==
The gust hits like a door swung by God. Your coat snaps, the chain hums, and the world tilts ten degrees before slamming back. You hold — boots wide, knuckles white, breath torn sideways out of you — and then it's past, and the fog streams on down the cliff without you.

When your hearing returns, the surf sounds pleased with itself. Above, dim and sudden, a yellow square resolves: the tower's lower window. Nearly there.

[[Through the door in the white base of the tower -> Tower Door]]

== Chain Crawl ==
You go up the way ivy does, slowly, on principle. Each step gets felt for, argued with, taken. It is humiliating and effective, and the wind has all the time in the world to work on a moving target.

It takes your collar at the fifth switchback and shakes you like a rat. Something leaves you — hat, lantern, dignity, depending on what you carried — and goes clattering away into the dark below. You hear the sea receive it.

~ set nerve = nerve - 1

[[Climb the rest on hands and knees -> The Lost Lantern]]

== The Lost Lantern ==
{{if has("storm lantern")}}
The wind finds the storm lantern's bail and plucks it from your grip with a tenderness that is worse than violence. You see it turn once against the fog, still absurdly upright, and then the dark below closes its hand.

~ drop "storm lantern"

You lean into the rock and breathe until the loss shrinks to something you can carry instead.
{{else}}
The gust that's been stalking you finally connects — a slap across the shoulders that sends you to one knee on the wet iron, palms skinned, cap gone somewhere below without even a splash to mark it.

The cliff took its toll. It does not apologise.
{{end}}

~ set nerve = nerve - 1

[[Push on to the white base of the tower -> Tower Door]]

== Tower Door ==
The tower's base is a drum of whitewashed stone, and its seaward door stands open — Mrs Voss left it that way, perhaps, or the wind has opinions about doors. Inside, a lamplight sways.

A girl is sitting on the step with her back to the jamb, a hurricane lamp between her boots and a covered basket by her feet. Seventeen, perhaps. Dark hair pinned badly. She looks up, and you see her decide not to cry.

"You'll be the nephew," she says. "Mara Voss. Edith was my gran." She nods at the doorway, at the cold spiral visible beyond it. "Somebody's got to sit with it the first night. That's what she always said. Somebody's got to sit with it."

{{if trust >= 2}}
She shifts along the step to make proper room, and the basket's smell of warm bread escapes for a moment.
{{end}}

* Climb to the watch room -> The Spiral Stair
* Try the iron door set in the seaward wall -> The Oil Store
* Sit down beside her on the step -> Mara at the Door
* (if turns() >= 60) The fog is thickening. Whatever tonight needs, it needs it upstairs -> The Spiral Stair

== The Oil Store ==
The iron door opens on a room the size of a confessional, stone-flagged, cold as a cellar. Wooden racks line one wall, and in them stand the tins: squat, sealed, stencilled KEEPER'S GRADE — PARAFFIN, each one a night's wages in oil. The smell is thick enough to chew. In fifty years this little cell has been the difference between ships coming home and ships not, and it smells like it knows.

{{if storeTins >= 2}}
Four tins wait in the rack. The rest of the shelf space wears pale rectangles of dust where other tins stood, other nights, other hands.
{{else}}
The rack stands empty. Nothing left in the store but the smell and the dust-shadows of everything that burned.
{{end}}

* (if has("brass key") and storeTins >= 2) Haul two tins up into the daylight -> Two More Tins
[[Back out to the base of the tower -> Tower Door]]

== Two More Tins ==
You load two tins, one through each elbow, and the weight of them rearranges your posture and your opinion of Mrs Voss simultaneously. Forty years she climbed this rock with these under her arms. The door bangs behind you; the fog presses politely at your back all the way to the stair.

~ set storeTins = storeTins - 2
~ take "tin of lamp oil", 2

[[Hump the burden up the spiral stair -> The Spiral Stair]]

== Mara at the Door ==
You settle onto the step. For a while neither of you says anything, which is its own kind of company. The hurricane lamp throws your two shadows huge against the whitewash, joined at the shoulder.

{{if visited("Mara at the Door") == 1}}
"She sat right here, you know," Mara says at last. "Every evening before she climbed. Gran said the tower won't startle on you if you let it get used to your weight on the step."
{{elif trust >= 2}}
This time she hands you bread without being asked, thick slices with butter gone sweaty in the basket. Eating together on a cold step is a faster friendship than most.
{{else}}
She watches the fog move over the harbour lights below like it's a programme she's seen before but hopes ends differently.
{{end}}

{{if trust >= 1}}
"I don't care what the solicitor's paper says," she adds quietly. "Tonight that light's ours. Yours and mine."
{{end}}

* (once) Ask her about her grandmother -> About Edda Voss
* (once) Promise her the light will burn till morning -> A Promise Made
* (once, if readJournal) Show her the journal you found -> The Journal Shared
* (once, if trust >= 2) Ask her to run two tins up the stair while you mind the flame -> Mara Carries Tins
* (once, if trust >= 2) Ask what she knows about the horn signals -> The Signal Pattern
* (if turns() >= 60) Squeeze her hand and go in; the tower is waiting -> Tower Door
[[Leave her to her vigil and go inside -> Tower Door]]

== About Edda Voss ==
"Gran?" Mara turns the hurricane lamp's wick down, the way you do when you're settling in to talk. "She taught me the lens before she taught me letters. Which way to wipe the glass, never in circles. How to hear weather in the buoy." A pause. "People think keeping a light is loneliness. It isn't. It's housework for something enormous. You feed it, you clean it, you sit up with it when it's poorly."

Her chin comes up. "She meant to train me next year. Now the relief boat brings some stranger Monday, and nobody will know the third rung sings in a crosswind."

~ set trust = trust + 1

[[Sit with her a moment longer -> Mara at the Door]]

== A Promise Made ==
You say it plainly, because anything fancier would break on her: the light will burn till morning. The Meridian will see it. You will be up there all night if that's what it takes.

Mara looks at you for a long moment, and whatever she was going to say about strangers and solicitors' papers, she folds it away and puts it in her pocket.

"All right," she says. "Then I'll be here at first light to see it out." She holds out her hand, and hers is a working hand, small and rough as a rope's end. You shake it.

~ set trust = trust + 1

[[Go in ready for anything -> Mara at the Door]]

== The Journal Shared ==
You bring out the cloth-bound journal and tell her where you found it, open on the desk like it was being read to.

Mara goes very still. Then she turns to the back page, the one you couldn't decipher, and laughs — one wet, surprised bark of a laugh.

"It's me. It's my handwriting. She made me copy out the horn patterns last spring, said a light that can't talk is just a candle in a jar." Her finger stops on a line. "'Tell whoever follows: three-and-one means Voss.' Gran." She wipes her face with her sleeve, briskly, businesslike. "Right. Now you know it properly."

~ set trust = trust + 1

[[Leave her the last slice of bread -> Mara at the Door]]

== Mara Carries Tins ==
She's up before you've finished agreeing, basket hooked over one arm, already reaching for your elbow. "Two at a time, elbows not hands, or you'll be visiting the third-step liar." And she's away into the tower's throat, lamp bobbing, boots quick on the iron.

You hear her all the way up — a knock, a scrape, her breath — and then she's back down, flushed and satisfied, and somewhere above you two more tins sit waiting in the lamp room's rack like answered prayers.

~ take "tin of lamp oil", 2

[[Thank her properly -> Mara at the Door]]

== The Signal Pattern ==
"The horn's a language," Mara says, standing, suddenly brisk. "Gran drilled me on it till I dreamed it. Listen: three blasts and one. Three-and-one means this light, this shore, Voss keeping. Any master who's sailed this coast twenty years knows it like his mother's voice." She taps out the rhythm on her thigh: bum, bum, bum, bumm. "You sound that when a ship's blind out there, and she'll know exactly whose shore she's leaning on, and which side her water is."

You say it back until she nods.

~ set knowsSignal = true

[[Fix the rhythm in your memory -> Mara at the Door]]

== The Spiral Stair ==
Inside, the tower is a cold throat of stone spiralling up into blackness. Iron stairs pinwheel around a central column hung with counterweights and the ghosts of pulleys. Your boots ring; the ring travels up ahead of you, announcing your arrival to the night shift.

{{if visited("The Lamp Room") > 2}}
Your legs have learned the rhythm now — riser, riser, landing, turn — and take it without consulting you. The climb that nearly killed your lungs at dusk has become almost contemplative: forty tons of masonry, and you ticking up through its middle like a second hand.
{{else}}
Halfway, the third step from the landing clangs hollow under your boot — Dune's liar step, loose on its bolts. You commit the sound to memory. On a night like tonight, a lie told once is a warning; told twice, a trap.
{{end}}

Wind moans somewhere above, thin as a flute.

[[Up to the watch room -> The Watch Room]]
[[Back down to the base -> Tower Door]]

== The Watch Room ==
The watch room is the tower's heart: a circular chamber panelled in pale pine gone honey-dark, a desk under the seaward window, a cupboard, a stool, and a potbellied stove that radiates the memory of fires rather than fire itself. Everything is squared away with the particular neatness of someone who expected to die elsewhere. Logbooks in a row. A folded chart. On the wall, mounted like a saint's picture, a framed photograph of a woman in a high collar, unsmiling, magnificent.

{{if visited("The Watch Room") > 1}}
The room feels different on a second visit — smaller, warmer, as if it has decided about you.
{{end}}

* Open the writing desk -> The Writing Desk
* Study the brass plate beside the window -> The Memorial Plate
* (once) Lift the storm lantern down from its ceiling hook -> Lantern From the Peg
* (if turns() >= 60) The stair creaks a reminder: the lamp keeps its own hours, not yours -> The Lamp Room
[[Up the ship's ladder to the lamp -> The Lamp Room]]
[[Down the spiral stair -> The Spiral Stair]]

== Lantern From the Peg ==
The storm lantern comes down off its hook on a length of sash cord, and it is a good one — brass-framed, glass smoked clean, the wick dry and waiting. Mrs Voss's spare, kept ready the way she kept everything: as if tonight were coming.

You light it from the stove's coals and hang the glow from your fist. The tower gives you a longer shadow to carry.

~ take "storm lantern"

[[Climb with the light swinging at your knee -> The Watch Room]]

== The Writing Desk ==
The desk is oak, scarred, orderly. An inkwell with the pen laid across it at a precise angle, as if its owner had been called away mid-word. A stack of supply requisitions, filled out in a copperplate hand, dated through the end of the month — through the end of her life. A drawer, fitted tight, the wood swollen with salt damp.

{{if seen("The Journal")}}
The place where the journal lay is a journal-shaped rectangle free of dust.
{{end}}

* (once) Force the swollen drawer -> The Deep Drawer
* (once) Take the cloth-bound journal lying open at the corner -> The Journal
[[Back to the centre of the room -> The Watch Room]]

== The Deep Drawer ==
You work the drawer with a heel-rocking pull that would have appalled its owner. It gives all at once and spills its secrets into the lamplight: spare glasses, a coil of fuse wire, a tin of peppermints fused into a single mint meteorite —

— and a box of matches, waxed and full, because Mrs Voss was not a woman who trusted darkness to behave.

~ take "box of matches"

[[Sweep everything back and shut the drawer -> The Writing Desk]]

== The Journal ==
The journal is cloth-bound, black, fat with decades. You mean to skim it. Instead you read.

The early pages are supply sums and weather, a clerk's soul. Then, somewhere around the war years, a voice surfaces — dry, exact, alive:

*A gull has learned to sit on the ventilator and scream when I sweep. I am paying it in biscuit to scream elsewhere.*

*Nine weeks of fog. The bell never stopped. Some nights I rang the bell back. Nobody will ever know this but the fog, and now the book.*

And near the end, in a shakier hand, half a page that reads like a will:

*Three-and-one is our hail, three-and-one the answer. Tell whoever follows: the light is not a job, it is a guest you keep. Feed it. Sit with it. It hates to be alone, and it is never, ever sentimental about the careless.*

~ take "keeper's journal"
~ set readJournal = true
~ set knowsSignal = true

[[Put it inside your coat, next to the letter -> The Writing Desk]]

== The Memorial Plate ==
Brass, screwed to the panelling where the light falls, polished by a century of sleeves: a tablet of names. Not decorative. This is the tower's own graveyard, kept indoors where the sea can't gloat over it.

{{for line in lostNames}}
In brass: {{line}}
{{end}}

Below the names, smaller, in the same confident capitals: THEY KEPT THE WATCH. KEEP IT.

* (once) Trace the newest letters with a fingertip -> Newest Name
[[Back to the window and its fog-grey light -> The Watch Room]]

== Newest Name ==
The bottom row is newer than the rest — recut, regilded, the letters still sharp enough to bite a careless fingertip. TOM CLEAVE, APPRENTICE, SIXTEEN, the same night as Maren Voss and the Long Hope. A boy sent up to fetch something. A woman who went down looking for him.

You take your finger off the brass gently, the way you'd close a door on sleepers.

[[Back to the plate -> The Memorial Plate]]

== The Lamp Room ==
The ship's ladder delivers you into the lantern: a glass drum at the crown of the tower, walled in curved panes and the great lens, which stands at the centre like the idol of some vast polite religion — brass ribs, a bullseye of cut crystal taller than you, everything built to gather one small flame and throw it twenty miles.

The lamp itself squats beneath the lens on its brass pedestal: reservoir, wick-holder, the spirit cup for priming. Cold. Unlit. The glass around you holds the fog at bay, and beyond the glass the world is a suggestion of darker grey.

{{Gauge(lamp)}}

{{if crackedGlass}}
One lower pane wears a crack like a frozen river. The lens still gathers, but the wind worries the flaw all night, a thin tick like a clock that suspects something.
{{end}}
{{if not flame}}
The wick sits dry in its holder, waiting to be somebody's.
{{end}}
{{if visited("The Lamp Room") > 3}}
You know this room now. Your boots know the two floorboards that rock. The room, you suspect, knows you back.
{{end}}

* (if not flame and lamp > 0) Prime the wick from the spirit cup and light it -> First Flame
* (if not flame and count("tin of lamp oil") > 0) Pour a tin and coax the wick back to life -> Wick Relit
